# 6. The Most Important BFS Concept: `levelSize`

```java
int levelSize = queue.size();
```

At the beginning of the `while` loop, the queue contains all nodes belonging to the current level.

We save that count in `levelSize`.

While processing those nodes, their children are added to the queue. Those children belong to the next level.

Therefore, we must process exactly `levelSize` nodes.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
    // Process current-level nodes
}
```

## The most important memory rule

```text
One while-loop iteration = one complete level

One for-loop iteration = one node in that level
```

After the `for` loop finishes:

```text
The queue contains nodes of the next level.
```

