# 13. LeetCode 111: Minimum Depth of Binary Tree

## Problem idea

Minimum depth means the shortest distance from the root to a **leaf node**.

A leaf node has:

```java
node.left == null && node.right == null
```

BFS processes shallower levels before deeper levels.

Therefore, the first leaf found by BFS gives the minimum depth.

## Code

```java
class Solution {

    public int minDepth(TreeNode root) {

        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root);

        int depth = 1;

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {

            int levelSize = queue.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {

                TreeNode node = queue.poll();

                // First leaf found is at the minimum depth.
                if (node.left == null && node.right == null) {
                    return depth;
                }

                if (node.left != null) {
                    queue.offer(node.left);
                }

                if (node.right != null) {
                    queue.offer(node.right);
                }
            }

            depth++;
        }

        return depth;
    }
}
```

## Important mistake to avoid

This is not always a leaf:

```java
node.left == null || node.right == null
```

A node is a leaf only when both children are null:

```java
node.left == null && node.right == null
```

