# 18. Common Mistakes

## Mistake 1: Not handling an empty tree

Correct:

```java
if (root == null) {
    return result;
}
```

Depending on the return type, you may return:

```java
return null;
return 0;
return result;
```

---

## Mistake 2: Using the changing queue size

Wrong:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < queue.size(); i++) {
```

The queue size changes when nodes are removed and children are added.

Correct:

```java
int levelSize = queue.size();

for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
```

---

## Mistake 3: Forgetting to add children

```java
if (node.left != null) {
    queue.offer(node.left);
}

if (node.right != null) {
    queue.offer(node.right);
}
```

Without this, BFS stops after processing the root.

---

## Mistake 4: Adding the level too early

Wrong:

```java
for (...) {
    currentLevel.add(node.val);
    result.add(currentLevel);
}
```

Correct:

```java
for (...) {
    currentLevel.add(node.val);
}

result.add(currentLevel);
```

Add the level only after it is completely processed.

---

## Mistake 5: Creating the level list outside the while loop

Wrong:

```java
List<Integer> currentLevel = new ArrayList<>();

while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
```

This reuses the same list for every level.

Correct:

```java
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {

    List<Integer> currentLevel = new ArrayList<>();
```

Every level gets a new list.

---

## Mistake 6: Starting maximum at zero

Wrong:

```java
int max = 0;
```

Correct:

```java
int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
```

Node values can be negative.

---

## Mistake 7: Confusing `Node` with `TreeNode`

Normal binary-tree problems generally use:

```java
TreeNode
```

LeetCode 116 and 117 use:

```java
Node
```

because these problems require:

```java
Node next;
```

---

## Mistake 8: Using a visited set for a normal tree

For a standard binary tree, a visited set is usually unnecessary.

```java
Set<TreeNode> visited = new HashSet<>();
```

is generally needed for graphs because graphs may contain cycles.

